# The build account may run commands as root without a password (D-033).
#
# Managed by ArchWork. paru refuses to run as root, so the AUR set is installed
# as archwork-build, and paru then reaches for root itself through sudo.
#
# This started as a list of four binaries, on the reasoning that a scoped grant
# is more legible than a blanket one. Two runs took that list apart: paru wants
# pacman, then makechrootpkg, then `install -dm755 /var/lib/aurbuild/x86_64`,
# and there is no reason to think that is the end of it. The list was not a
# boundary, it was a guess about paru's internals that failed twice.
#
# It was never a security boundary either, which is the part that matters.
# `pacman -U` runs the installed package's own scripts as root, so the first
# entry on that list already granted everything the rest of it withheld. A
# narrow-looking rule that grants root anyway is worse than a wide one: it
# invites a reader to believe in a limit that is not there.
#
# So this is what it always was. Read it as: the build account is root, without
# a password, whenever something invokes it. D-033 records why that is
# acceptable here, and D-018 is amended to say it is no longer true that this
# account holds no privileges.
archwork-build ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: ALL
